# Adopt and replace MCP catalogs and access policies

This guide covers five installation workflows. Use an Obot **Admin or Owner**
API-scoped token. Configure the provider as described on its landing page.
The examples are module snippets; declare `obot-platform/obot` in the root
module. These resources manage the default MCP catalog's sources and the
policies shown under **MCP Servers > Access Policies**. vMCP profiles are
separate and are outside this guide.

## 1. Bring shipped catalog entries under management

Git continues to own entry definitions. Terraform manages which sources Obot
synchronizes; it does not take ownership of individual Git-owned entries.
There can be only one `obot_mcp_catalog_sources` resource per installation.
The source collection is authoritative, so include every desired source.

First inspect the current configuration using read-only data sources:

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/obot_mcp_catalog/data-source.tf" }}

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/obot_mcp_access_rules/data-source.tf" }}

Run `terraform plan`, then use `terraform console` to inspect:

```text
data.obot_mcp_catalog.default.sources
data.obot_mcp_catalog.default.source_git_credential_ids
data.obot_mcp_catalog.default.entries
data.obot_mcp_access_rules.current.rules
```

Copy the live source URLs and Git credential references into your variable
values. If the data is not yet available in console, apply the data-source-only
configuration first; doing so makes no changes in Obot. Save those values as
your desired configuration rather than continuously assigning a resource's
inputs from a live lookup of that same resource.

Add the source resource and import block:

{{ tffile "examples/guides/catalog-adoption/main.tf" }}

```shell
terraform plan -out=adopt.tfplan
terraform apply adopt.tfplan
```

Review the plan: adoption should show an import with **no remote updates**.
Import only reads existing configuration. A differing URL or credential map
means the plan also changes Obot, so correct the configuration first if you
intend a pure adoption. Complete HTTPS URLs are required; include the desired
Git ref and subdirectory. Existing legacy inline credentials for retained
sources are preserved by Obot and are never exposed in Terraform state.
To reference shared Git credentials, create them in Obot and supply their IDs.

You can instead remove the import block and run:

```shell
terraform import obot_mcp_catalog_sources.default default
terraform plan
```

## 2. Replace shipped entries with your own catalog

After adoption, remove the source import block and change the desired sources:

```hcl
mcp_catalog_sources = [
  "https://github.com/example/obot-catalog/tree/main/mcp",
]
mcp_catalog_git_credential_ids = {}
```

Include credential references for private sources. Apply a reviewed plan:

```shell
terraform plan -out=replace-catalog.tfplan
terraform apply replace-catalog.tfplan
```

The provider updates sources, explicitly requests synchronization, and waits
up to five minutes for a new successful sync. Obot removes entries belonging
to removed sources and loads entries from the replacement sources.
API-created entries are preserved. A source URL participates in entry identity;
references to old entry IDs, including references in existing vMCPs, are not
rewritten automatically.

To remove all source-managed entries without adding replacements, retain the
resource and set `mcp_catalog_sources = []`. Destroying the source resource
also clears its sources and credential references. It preserves the default
catalog; it does not restore shipped defaults. On Obot v0.26.3 and the tested
main image, keeping the default catalog prevents startup from re-adding the
shipped source list. The provider does not change the server's startup settings.

A synchronization error fails apply. Obot may retain old entries when any
source fails, so a failed apply is not proof that replacement completed.
Inspect `data.obot_mcp_catalog.default.sync_errors` or the Obot UI, correct
URLs, credentials, or connectivity, and plan again. After a transient error
with no configuration change, request another sync from the Obot UI, wait
for it to complete successfully, then run `terraform plan` again. A no-op
Terraform plan does not initiate a sync.

## 3. Get the current authentication provider

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/obot_current_auth_provider/data-source.tf" }}

The data source exposes `id`, `name`, `namespace`, and `group_id_prefix`.
It reads public metadata, never authentication-provider credentials. Use the
prefix when interpreting provider group IDs; it does not enumerate groups
or users.

The API does not supply an authoritative active-provider flag. This lookup
requires exactly one configured provider after excluding staged replacements.
It errors if none or multiple match and requires an Admin or Owner token so
staging information is visible. It describes installation configuration,
not the authentication provider originally used to obtain an API key.

## 4. Bring the Everything MCP access policy under management

Find the rule in `data.obot_mcp_access_rules.current.rules`. The shipped ID
is normally `acr1-everything`; inspect the actual instance rather than
selecting by display name, which is not unique.

{{ tffile "examples/data-sources/obot_mcp_access_rule/data-source.tf" }}

Copy the returned display name, `subjects`, and `resources` into the following
example's variable values. On the tested Obot versions, the shipped Everything
policy grants the built-in `admin` group access to the wildcard resource.
Other installations may grant everyone using `{ type = "selector", id = "*" }`
as the subject. **Everything is a display name, not a guarantee of everyone
access.** Import preserves whichever subjects actually exist.

{{ tffile "examples/guides/access-policy-adoption/main.tf" }}

Use the same plan-and-apply adoption flow as above. The plan should import
`default/<rule-id>` without updating or replacing the existing policy.
Import makes no policy API writes. After adoption, remove the import block;
future changes to the resource update that policy in place.

CLI import is also supported:

```shell
terraform import obot_mcp_access_rule.everything default/acr1-everything
terraform plan
```

Generated policies can be inspected through data sources but cannot be
managed by the resource. Manage their owning Obot object instead.

## 5. Remove the Everything MCP access policy

Import it first as above. Remove **both** its resource block and its import
block. Also remove any singular data source or reference that requires the
deleted rule to exist. Then run:

```shell
terraform plan -out=remove-everything.tfplan
terraform apply remove-everything.tfplan
```

The plan should show deletion of that one `obot_mcp_access_rule`. Other
policies remain in place. Grants are additive, so deleting Everything does
not revoke access granted by another policy. On the tested Obot versions,
startup does not recreate this seed policy while the default MCP catalog exists.

`terraform state rm` merely stops Terraform tracking the rule and leaves it
in Obot. A `removed` block with `destroy = false` also leaves it in place.
To preview deletion of every managed object in this workspace, use
`terraform plan -destroy`; `terraform destroy` performs that broader teardown
and is unnecessary for removing just this policy.
